  "notes": "Teen-prodigy torch voice that became an outright pop-crossover engine. The research documents \"two grammars in one catalogue\" as an explicit split, which is the only reason this model authors moods at all: (a) \"1950s-revival country: slow 2&4 with cross-stick, brushes and a swung 8th feel\" and (b) \"pop-country: 2&4 with programmed 16th hats, claps on the backbeat and a live snare layered on top.\" Each mood therefore carries its own melody and harmony, not just a feel — the torch lane is slow, sparse and melisma-heavy over I–IV–V with a swung dominant, the pop lane is dense 16th phrasing over the vi–IV–I–V and i–VI–III–VII loops the entry names. The 115–140 four-on-the-floor club remixes are explicitly \"*not* modelled here,\" so the band stops at 110. Melody is the entry's own description: \"the widest-interval, most melisma-heavy in the 90s female cohort — long sustained notes with a wide, slow vibrato at phrase ends, and yodel-adjacent octave breaks as a signature ornament,\" which is the raised leap share and the high octave-jump probability. ⚠ Boundary flag from the research: \"'Blue' itself is a swung torch ballad — `country-shuffle` may want that specific groove.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high) and the per-track readings are marked [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The mood BPM bands are derived rather than quoted: the entry gives one band for the country masters (62–110) plus four per-track readings (62, 80, 92, 98), so 62–92 and 80–110 are this author’s split of that band across the two documented lanes — and the club remixes at 115–140 are excluded because the entry says they are \"*not* modelled here.\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the swing amount on the torch mood, the clap offset and the mood weights — the entry states that both lanes exist but not their share, and 0.58 / 5:4 are this author's reading of it.",
